meta {
  name: 05 Collect Sample
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/accession/order-samples/{{sampleId}}/collect
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{businessAccessToken}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "tubeType": "EDTA (Purple)",
    "collectedAt": "2026-07-20T09:30:00.000Z",
    "notes": "Collected at front desk",
    "attachmentUrl": "https://files.example.com/collect-slip.pdf"
  }
}

tests {
  test("transition applied (or rejected by the state machine)", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.be.oneOf([200, 201, 422]);
  });
}

docs {
  Collect Sample (§A.10.1) — **New / Hold / Repeat → Collected**. `tubeType` is
  **required** and is **free text** (a string of up to 100 characters): the Collect
  modal offers a dropdown of the configured tube types, but the API does **not**
  check the value against that list. It is informational only and does not affect
  Store, Return, Forward, Repeat or the analyzer. `collectedAt`
  defaults to now when omitted. Records `collectedBy` = caller. Illegal from other
  statuses → 422 `INVALID_SAMPLE_TRANSITION`.

  The sample **barcode is generated here, on collection** (sequential +
  grouping-aware, one shared value per group) — barcodes are **no longer**
  assigned at order creation. Plain Collect barcodes the sample just like
  Collect & Print. Collects of one tenant that run at the same moment queue on the
  tenant's sample counter, so two tests of one barcode group collected together
  share one barcode instead of each getting its own.

  Every §A.10 modal also accepts optional `notes` + `attachmentUrl` (URL only —
  no upload endpoint), recorded on the history row.
}
